{:edb/db-before-t 1365 :edb/db-after-t 1366 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534678 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:01:19.419Z" 13194139534678 true] [17592186082858 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n$u|= 300\\frac{m}{min}; = 300\\frac{m}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s}; = 300\\frac{m}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s}; = \\frac{300}{60} \\cdot \\frac{m}{s}; = 5m/s$\n- The penny falls for $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n&= (5)(2) + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 10 + 19.6 \\\\\n&= 29.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $29.6\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139534678 false] [17592186082858 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{60}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The penny falls for $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =10+19.6 \\\\  & =29.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $29.6\\,\\textrm{m}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186082859 1100 "A stone is projected vertically downward from a height of $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ with a velocity of  $14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the stone to hit the ground?" 13194139534678 true] [17592186082859 1100 "A stone is projected vertically downward from a height of $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ with a velocity of $14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. How long will it take for the stone to hit the ground?" 13194139534678 false] [17592186082859 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the stone is $u=14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$.\n- The stone covers the distance of $s=19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19.6 &= (14.7)t + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t^{2} \\\\\n4(4.9) &= 3(4.9)t + (4.9)t^{2} \\\\\n4(4.9) &= 3(4.9)t + (4.9)t^{2} \\\\\n4 &= 3t + t^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} + 3t - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 4)(t - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -4, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the stone hits the ground after $t=1$ second." 13194139534678 false] [17592186082859 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the stone is $u=14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$\n\n- The stone covers the distance of $s=19.6\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6 & =(14.7)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 4(4.9) & =3(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 4\\cancel{(4.9)} & =3\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =3t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t-4 & =0 \\\\ (t+4)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the stone hits the ground after $t=1$ second." 13194139534678 true] [17592186083055 1100 "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}$. It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" 13194139534678 false] [17592186083055 1100 "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}.$ It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" 13194139534678 true] [17592186083055 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n(110.4) &= u(4) + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\\n110.4 &= 4u + 78.4 \\\\\n32 &= 4u \\\\\nu &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was\n\n$$\nu= 8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}\n$$" 13194139534678 false] [17592186083055 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (110.4) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 110.4 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 32 & =4u \\\\ u & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was $u= 8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186083294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302885 13194139534678 true] [17592186083294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302923 13194139534678 true] [17592186083294 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302926 13194139534678 true] [17592186083295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302892 13194139534678 true] [17592186083295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302899 13194139534678 true] [17592186083295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302906 13194139534678 true] [17592186083295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302929 13194139534678 true] [17592186083295 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302932 13194139534678 true] [17592186083296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302878 13194139534678 true] [17592186083296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302913 13194139534678 true] [17592186083296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302920 13194139534678 true] [17592186083296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302935 13194139534678 true] [17592186083296 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302942 13194139534678 true] [17592186083393 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534678 true] [17592186083393 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*" 13194139534678 false] [17592186083393 1100 "A ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139534678 true] [17592186083393 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball falls for $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0)(3)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9(9) \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186083533 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534678 true] [17592186083533 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9km/min$. It takes the object $4s$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*\n\nThe height of the building is {{field-1}} $m$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-for-initial-velocity\"></a>" 13194139534678 false] [17592186083533 1100 "An object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9\\,\\text{km/min}.$ It takes the object $4\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe height of the building is{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186083533 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The object falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(15)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =60+(9.8)(8) \\\\  & =138.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $138.4\\,\\text{m}$ high." 13194139534678 true] [17592186302878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302879 13194139534678 true] [17592186302878 1089 "q-10510" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302878 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534678 true] [17592186302878 1100 "A ball is dropped vertically downward from a height of $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the ball to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302878 1113 #uuid "a2086c80-ba1e-5c33-a529-5c1e9cecd9e9" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302878 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=44.1\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{44.1}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the ball hits the ground after $t=3$ seconds." 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302880 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302881 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302882 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302883 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302884 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1041 "selection" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302882 13194139534678 true] [17592186302879 1110 #uuid "8aa3b800-b3d4-5e34-8f5a-392ee6ed9fb0" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302880 1034 "2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 tru