in the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.25)(1.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(1.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.375+11.025 \\\\  & =11.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $11.4\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302928 13194139534678 true] [17592186302927 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534678 true] [17592186302927 1041 "field-1" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302927 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302928 13194139534678 true] [17592186302927 1110 #uuid "7c2019f8-04e3-54da-8d10-8d9ce08239f4" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302928 1034 "11.4" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302928 1111 #uuid "b50a3ac6-1033-5c42-b49b-75b4ccd6dd8f" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302929 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302930 13194139534678 true] [17592186302929 1089 "q-251595" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534678 true] [17592186302929 1100 "A pear is projected vertically down from a height of $1.5\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $0.5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the pear? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302929 1113 #uuid "c74668ee-26b0-5adf-a3cb-6d4db7a860f9" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302929 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The pear falls for $t=0.5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The pear covers the distance of $s=1.5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (1.5) & =u(0.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(0.5)^{2} \\\\ 1.5 & =0.5u+1.225 \\\\ 0.275 & =0.5u \\\\ u & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the pear was $0.55\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302931 13194139534678 true] [17592186302930 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534678 true] [17592186302930 1041 "field-1" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302930 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302931 13194139534678 true] [17592186302930 1110 #uuid "04941cbf-3bd7-5a54-ba23-49ec1dfc88a4" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302931 1034 "0.55" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302931 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302931 1111 #uuid "0e1cf6ed-39e5-58fd-b65b-40b6d02b58bd" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302932 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302933 13194139534678 true] [17592186302932 1089 "q-251707" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302932 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534678 true] [17592186302932 1100 "A ball is projected vertically down from a height of $4.5\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $30\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the ball?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302932 1113 #uuid "e9955871-7a2b-5833-a349-093c6e6531aa" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302932 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=30\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the ball falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =4.5\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =4.5\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =4.5\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =4.5\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =4\\,500\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (4\\,500) & =u(30)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(30)^{2} \\\\ 4\\,500 & =30u+4\\,410 \\\\ 90 & =30u \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was  $u=3\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302934 13194139534678 true] [17592186302933 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534678 true] [17592186302933 1041 "field-1" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302933 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302934 13194139534678 true] [17592186302933 1110 #uuid "ef2022cf-c5ee-5a72-92f2-e6d32d2f9822" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302934 1034 "3" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302934 1111 #uuid "782922f8-a5b1-5bd8-9b87-9200c5878647" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302935 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302936 13194139534678 true] [17592186302935 1089 "q-31409" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302935 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534678 true] [17592186302935 1100 "A brick is dropped vertically downward from a height of $176.4\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the brick to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302935 1113 #uuid "4de376bd-96ce-5105-b48d-6021ccb45d98" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302935 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the brick is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The brick covers the distance of $s=176.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{176.4}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =36 \\\\ t & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the brick hits the ground after $t=6$ seconds." 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302937 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302938 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302939 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302940 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302941 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1041 "selection" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302938 13194139534678 true] [17592186302936 1110 #uuid "c638cad5-5250-5ab1-a5ba-d64db75a6f71" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302937 1034 "4\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302937 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302937 1111 #uuid "dbe28e4a-fccb-57f6-85e9-8cf93b6cd87e" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302938 1034 "6\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302938 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302938 1111 #uuid "53516ac7-1295-5dbc-ba12-76cd5f29e4da" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302939 1034 "3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302939 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302939 1111 #uuid "e3601c99-1db7-52e7-83fb-8f4a5fd101fe" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302940 1034 "5\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302940 1111 #uuid "fd476e8d-6269-570a-9eea-4613747ce6c3" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302941 1034 "7\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302941 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302941 1111 #uuid "59be3aca-dbf7-55ab-8948-c1cf91e2aa95" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302942 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302943 13194139534678 true] [17592186302942 1089 "q-31411" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534678 true] [17592186302942 1100 "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $88.2\\,\\text{m}$ with a velocity of  $141.12\\,\\text{km/h}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302942 1113 #uuid "bd7ba8c8-a475-5dfc-9236-81e389c6fb25" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302942 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{60\\,\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{60\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{{60}^{2}\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =39.2\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=88.2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}88.2 & =(39.2)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 18(4.9) & =8(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 18\\cancel{(4.9)} & =8\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8t-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-18)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+72}}{2} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-8\\pm 11.6619}{2} \\\\  & ≈-9.83,\\,1.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $1.83\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302944 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302945 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302946 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302947 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302948 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1041 "selection" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302946 13194139534678 true] [17592186302943 1110 #uuid "c49fa83e-beed-596e-96f7-f02661044ac3" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302944 1034 "3.26\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302944 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302944 1111 #uuid "b3a894f9-4256-556b-a6e2-d3f67fc25f80" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302945 1034 "5.56\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302945 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534678 true] [17592186302945 1111 #uuid "c31a4c43-b104-5208-a79f-e7f98649f40d" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302946 1034 "1.83\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534678 true] [17592186302946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045